  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Shower and thunderstorm activity will continue each afternoon \n   through Tuesday.\n\n-  Warming and drying latter part of next week under a ridge of\n   high pressure.\n\nVisible on satellite imagery this afternoon is a circulation of\nlow pressure off the coast of California, along with a smaller,\nweaker circulation near the Olympic Peninsula. These low pressure\nsystems to the west are keeping the Northern Rockies in southwest\nflow, resulting in the transport of moist air from the Pacific \ninto our region. For this afternoon, that means afternoon showers \nand thunderstorms will develop as they did yesterday. The \ngeographic focus for thunderstorm formation will be north-central \nIdaho (including Lemhi County), west-central Montana (including \nthe Bitterroot Valley), and southwest Montana (including Butte). \nHigh resolution models have backed off somewhat for convection \nover northwest Montana (including Kalispell and Glacier Park) from\nprevious runs, but certainly not ruling out a heavy rainer or \ntwo. Region-wide, model soundings indicate some deep instability \nwith modest shear conducive to heavy rain. The lower atmosphere \nlooks dry enough to support outflow gusts of 30-40 mph. Frequent \nlightning and small hail will round out the list of threats from \nthis afternoon's round of convection. \n\nOn Monday, the southwesterly moisture feed will continue, bringing\nanother round of convective showers in the afternoon. The\ngeographical focus will also remain north-central Idaho, along\nwith west-central and southwest Montana. Upper level flow \nflattens out on Tuesday, becoming more westerly, as the Northern \nRockies sits atop a broad ridge of high pressure over the \nsouthwestern U.S. through midweek. Expect increased shower\nactivity and westerly winds. That ridge amplifies later in the \nweek, putting the Northern Rockies under the ridge and into drier \nconditions and above-normal temperatures for Thursday and Friday.",
